# +Point, -Surface
#   * <module> The set-free series: a loop of cheap library calls, for the scope of compilation
#
# A family of the set-free series for the scope of compilation, whose
# largest gain is compiling more of a program and so avoiding the cost of
# the FFI. A point is a closed pure
# lambda program, no set in it, whose answer is one binary numeral.
#
# THE PROGRAM is an iterative Fibonacci over binary numerals, a loop the
# program writes itself around the library's addition:
#
#     fib_iter n a b = n a (\p. fib_iter p b (plus a b))
#
# n a unary numeral, a and b binary numerals, plus the attractor
# merge_binary_plus of corpora/contest_attractors.pl; fib_iter n 0 1 is
# fib(n). The loop makes n calls of plus on numbers of up to about 0.7 n
# bits:
# - a setting that replaces each call of plus by native addition converts
#   both arguments from Scott numerals and the result back at every call,
#   about n times the bits, a cost at the boundary between the calculus and
#   native code;
# - a setting that compiles the whole loop keeps a and b native throughout
#   and converts once, at the end, as native Prolog does.
#
# THE POINTS: a DIRECT and a RESPELLED twin of each size, and a NEAR MISS, a
# proved trap for compiling without checking that values are canonical: a
# loop that puts the constructor even around zero n times,
#
#     double_iter n x = n x (\p. double_iter p (even x)),   x = 0,
#
# whose normal form is the non-canonical tree even(even(... zero)). A
# setting that represents binary values as native integers answers the
# canonical zero, another normal form, outside S(P).
#
# TARGET ALGORITHMS, the specific known algorithms the problem targets, each
# with its prior work:
# - unboxed data representations chosen by the compiler, so that values in a
#   compiled region stay in machine form: X. Leroy, Unboxed Objects and
#   Polymorphic Typing, POPL 1992, doi:10.1145/143165.143205;
# - compiling a hot loop as a whole, with its values specialised to their
#   types: A. Gal et al., Trace-based Just-in-Time Type Specialization for
#   Dynamic Languages, PLDI 2009, doi:10.1145/1542476.1542528;
# - recognising the library's addition inside the program (equality
#   saturation, Tate et al., POPL 2009, doi:10.1145/1480881.1480915; call
#   restoration).
#
# THE SNAPSHOT is the canonical numeral of fib(n) for a Fibonacci point and
# the tree of n evens around zero for a near miss. PROOF. The program is
# pure, so S(P) = {N} for its normal form N. By
# induction on n, fib_iter n a b reduces to the numeral of F(n) for a = F(0)
# and b = F(1) shifted, plus on canonical numerals giving the canonical sum
# (prolog/gezi/catalogue_replacement.pl, "THE ENTRIES' INDUCTIONS
# (ii)"); double_iter n 0 reduces to even applied n times to zero, a normal
# form that is no canonical numeral for n >= 1, so it differs from the
# canonical zero.
# LIMITS: the reduction family's, 10 s, 256 MB, 256 MB.

#   scope_point(?Point, ?Loop, ?Spelling, ?Steps)
#
# The spelling is a constructor of schema/spelling, direct or respelled.
# Each other text is from_cbor of a string literal, a Scott list of bytes,
# imported from corpora/library/text/cbor. Each number is from_cbor of an
# integer literal, a Scott integer, imported from corpora/library/integer/cbor.
# FIXME: the imports project "corpora" https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1923
# off the variable gezi, the name of the project root's own
# directory; it breaks if that directory is renamed.
λ export
. (λtuple. tuple λmixin. mixin mixin)
λ omega
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "list")
λ nil cons _ _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "text") "cbor")
λ text_from_cbor _ _ _
. (λtuple. tuple λ. text_from_cbor λx. x)
λ text
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "integer") "cbor")
λ integer_from_cbor _
. (λtuple. tuple λ. integer_from_cbor λx. x)
λ integer
. omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "problems") "schema") "spelling")
λ direct respelled
. export
λ
. cons
  ( λ _scope_point
    . _scope_point (text "set_free_scope_small_direct") (text "fibonacci") direct λ. integer 10
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _scope_point
    . _scope_point (text "set_free_scope_small_respelled") (text "fibonacci") respelled λ. integer 10
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _scope_point
    . _scope_point (text "set_free_scope_small_near_miss") (text "doubling") direct λ. integer 10
  ) nil
